---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'

import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import AsciiRule from '../components/AsciiRule.astro'
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro'
import { skillGroups } from '../data/skills'
import { site } from '../site.config'

/**
 * Home. Who this is, what this is made of, and what has been built.
 *
 * THE VISUAL LANGUAGE IS NOT THE SIMULATION. The rounded frames, the monospace grid,
 * the `ch` discipline, the elevation ramp and the `#` marker are the identity and all of
 * them stay. What is gone is the theatre: the `user@host:~$` prompt, the command names,
 * the numbered history, the `ls` table, and the blinking caret. A page that pretends to
 * be a terminal is a page about pretending to be a terminal, and this one has enough to
 * say without it. What is left is a site that looks the way it was built and reads like
 * a portfolio rather than a transcript.
 *
 * THE ORDER IS STILL THE ARGUMENT: who I am, what I have built, and what I work with.
 * The rail used to sit in the middle carrying build-time facts about the website; it
 * carries the skills taxonomy now, because three of those four lines were about the site
 * and one restated the Projects section underneath it. See the note on the skills rail
 * below for what that cost and why it was worth it.
 *
 * There is no project data in this file. `getCollection` reads every markdown file in
 * `src/content/projects/`, so adding a project adds a card here without touching this
 * page — which is the whole point of the content layer.
 *
 * Sort order is `featured` first, then the author's `order`, then newest first. The
 * tiebreak matters: two projects with the same `order` should not swap places between
 * builds.
 */

const projects = (await getCollection('projects')).sort(
  (a, b) =>
    Number(b.data.featured) - Number(a.data.featured) ||
    a.data.order - b.data.order ||
    b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf(),
)

const hasProjects = projects.length > 0

/**
* The intro, in prose. Translated, not drafted.
 *
 * PROVENANCE. Every claim here came from the owner answering two questions, not from
 * inference, and the previous version of this text was a guess dressed as a claim:
 *
 *   1. The three-beat performance line is his. He was asked what kind of problem he
 *      wants to solve and answered that the goal is to fix bad performance, to push
 *      speed and consumption to their limit, and that this is WHY he went further into
 *      Rust and GPUI. The first two beats are corroborated by the CV, not by me:
 *      "enhancing system processing speeds" under Technical Support Specialist, and
 *      "improving system response times" under Full Stack Developer. So the thread runs
 *      through both jobs and both personal projects on the record, and the causality is
 *      his: he picked the problem, and the stack followed from it.
 *
 *   2. The Reborn paragraph is his, near-verbatim in substance. He was asked for the
 *      hardest thing he had to resolve, and the answer was not a bug at all: the first
 *      version was too ambitious to solve anything and ended badly, so he rebuilt it
 *      from zero knowing his limits, and this time it did its job. The consequence is
 *      that the project's name is the lesson, which no CV line could carry.
 *
 * WHAT IS STILL MINE: the English, and the order. He answered in Spanish and this is a
 * translation, which preserves his claims and loses his cadence. If it does not sound
 * like him, it is because it is not yet his English.
 *
 * No new facts are introduced here. That is what makes prose safe to write on a page
 * whose argument is that its numbers are real.
 */
const intro = [
  'Performance is the problem I keep coming back to. In technical support it was making systems stop being slow. As a full-stack developer it was response times. Now I work in Rust and GPUI because that is where speed and resource consumption are actually decided.',
  'Sh_Images Reborn is named Reborn because the first version failed, and not because of a bug. It was too ambitious to solve anything, so I threw it away and rebuilt it from zero, this time knowing what I was able to finish. Both projects below come from that habit: the constraints are measurable, and the numbers come off the reports rather than out of a README.',
]

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * The skills rail.
 *
 * It reads `skillGroups` and nothing else, which is the point: the taxonomy is
 * written once in `src/data/skills.ts` and both this rail and the About page render it,
 * so a skill cannot be listed in one place and missing from the other.
 *
 * THIS REPLACED A FACTS BLOCK, and the three lines it held were the reason it went.
 * `Built` and `Toolchain` were facts about the WEBSITE — which Astro version compiled
 * these bytes, on which Node, under which package manager — sitting on a page whose
 * subject is a person. `Projects` (`2 · active 2 · other 0`) restated the section
 * directly beneath it. Of the four, only `Focus` was a claim about the owner, and a
 * skills taxonomy says that and more.
 *
 * The honest cost, stated rather than buried: the site no longer prints its own
 * toolchain anywhere. That was a real flex and it was the only place the versions were
 * visible. They are still in `package.json` and in this repository's history, which is
 * where a reader who cares about them will actually look.
 *
 * FOUR GROUPS, AND THE RAIL IS NOT STICKY. Measured on the built site at a 351.56px
 * rail, the four groups come to 662.28px, and a sticky element needs `top` plus its own
 * height in the viewport or its bottom is unreachable while it is parked. 101 + 662 =
 * 763px, which clears a 768px laptop by five pixels and fails in anything shorter. So
 * this rail scrolls with the page. The case study keeps its sticky rail because a table
 * of contents is short and staying reachable is the entire reason to want one.
 *
 * THERE IS NO COUNT HERE, and there used to be a `26 items in 4 groups` line. A count
 * earns its place by telling the reader something the list does not already say, and
 * a rail that prints twelve lines of names without enumerating them does not: the
 * reader has no way to know whether they are looking at the whole taxonomy or an
 * excerpt, and the honest answer was to remove the count rather than to keep a number
 * that admits the gap. The full list is on the About page, where all 26 are visible
 * one by one. The `skillCount` reduction went with the line; Astro does not warn about
 * an unused binding, so it would have sat there forever.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * What the owner is doing right now, in one sentence.
 *
 * Both halves are already stated on this site rather than asserted here: Sh_Images
 * Reborn is a finished, released viewer that is `status: active` and still being
 * maintained, and Sh_Nexus says in its own words that it is in progress. The line is
 * written, not derived — `status: active` cannot tell a finished project from an
 * unfinished one, so there is nothing in the collection to compute this from.
 *
 * It is deliberately the quietest line in the intro: chrome size on the muted ramp, no
 * status light and no "available for work" badge, because whether the owner wants to
 * advertise availability is a decision he has not made yet. The intro is unframed, so
 * the surface here is `base`, where `subtext0` measures 7.37:1.
 */
const now =
  'maintaining a released desktop image viewer, and building a chat client and server alongside it.'
---

<BaseLayout title={`${site.name} — ${site.role}`} description={site.tagline}>
  <div class="page">
    <div class="hero">
      <header class="hero__text">
        <h1 class="hero__name">{site.name}</h1>
        <p class="hero__role">{site.role}</p>
        <p class="hero__tagline">{site.tagline}</p>
      </header>

      <img
        class="hero__portrait"
        src="/portrait.jpg"
        alt={site.name}
        width="360"
        height="450"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />

      <div class="intro__body">
        {intro.map((paragraph) => (
          <p class="intro__para">{paragraph}</p>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="body">
      <div class="body__main">
        <p class="now">
          <span class="now__label">currently</span>
          <span class="now__sep" aria-hidden="true">│</span>
          <span class="now__text">{now}</span>
        </p>

        <section class="section" id="projects">
          <AsciiRule as="div" />
          <h2 class="section__title">Projects</h2>

          {
            hasProjects ? (
              <ul class="cards" role="list">
                {projects.map((project) => (
                  <li>
                    <ProjectCard project={project} />
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            ) : (
              <p class="cards__note">
                No projects yet. Create one markdown file in <code>src/content/projects/</code>.
              </p>
            )
          }
        </section>
      </div>

      <aside class="body__rail">
        <section class="section" id="skills">
          <AsciiRule as="div" />
          <h2 class="section__title">Skills</h2>

          <div class="groups">
            {
              skillGroups.map((group) => (
                <section class="group">
                  <h3 class="label">{group.label}</h3>
                  <ul class="group__items" role="list">
                    {group.items.map((item) => (
                      <li class="group__item">{item}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </section>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </section>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ---- The page ---------------------------------------------------------------
     A stack of two blocks with a real gap between them, sized in the chrome's own
     leading so the vertical rhythm belongs to the character grid rather than to a
     guessed pixel value. Each block is a grid, because a block's heading and its
     content are two rows of one thing rather than two elements that happen to be
     adjacent.

     EVERY SINGLE-COLUMN GRID HERE DECLARES ITS TRACK, and that is load-bearing rather
     than stylistic. A grid with no `grid-template-columns` has one implicit `auto`
     track, and an `auto` track is sized by its content — which is a trap on this site
     specifically, because `Panel` and `AsciiRule` both hold runs of 256 box-drawing
     characters that are clipped rather than counted. An `auto` track asks for the
     MAX-CONTENT size of that run, `fr` does not cap the answer, and the whole content
     column measured 2304px inside a 773px shell. `minmax(0, 1fr)` is the fix: the track
     is then resolved against the container's definite width, the long runs clip as they
     were always meant to, and the page stops opening sideways. Measured before and
     after, in a headless browser. */
  .page {
    --block-gap: calc(var(--leading-chrome) * 2em);

    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    row-gap: var(--block-gap);
  }

  .section {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  /* A bare rule, then the heading, then the content. The rule is the section break and
     the heading is the label, which is the same grammar `.prose h2` uses, so a section
     on this page and a section inside a case study read as one family. `cols` is 88,
     the shell's own cell count, so the rule is exactly as wide as the frame it divides
     and `min(88ch, 100%)` falls back to the container below that. It has to track
     `--shell-cols` in `BaseLayout.astro`; a smaller number is a rule that stops short
     of the frame. */
  .section :global(.rule) {
    margin-block-end: 0.7em;
  }

  /* 28px, uppercase and tracked. The size step is real; case, weight and tracking are
     what make it a heading on a greyscale display, which is why all four are here. */
  .section__title {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin-block-end: 1.2em;
  }

  /* ---- The page grid ----------------------------------------------------------
     THE WHOLE POINT OF THE CHANGE, and the arithmetic that produced it.

     The shell used to be 88 cells — exactly one reading column, because
     `--measure-prose` is 66ch at 20px and 66 x 20 = 88 x 15. A page that is exactly as
     wide as its prose has nowhere to put a second thing, so every block after the first
     became a full-width band the reader had to scroll past to reach the next one. The
     owner asked for a page that is wider across the screen and less linear downward,
     and the naive answer — widen the shell, leave the prose alone — is the one that
     does not work:

        shell 130 cells          = 1142.58px
        content 94 cells         =  826.19px
        prose capped at 66ch     =  773.44px
        DEAD SPACE                            52.75px

     The paragraph sits 52.75px short of the frame around it, left-aligned, on every
     block on the page. That is not a wider page, it is a broken one.

     So the content track stays at 88 cells and the new width is spent on a RAIL:

        content  88 cells =  773.44px = 66ch at 20px exactly  <- unchanged, by design
        gutter    2 cells =   17.58px
        rail     40 cells =  351.56px
        shell   130 cells = 1142.58px

     1ch at `--text-base` measured in a headless browser is 8.7891px, not the nominal
     9px, and every figure above uses the measured one.

     THE BREAKPOINT IS THE SMALLEST VIEWPORT THAT STILL FITS THE GRID, and it is
     derived rather than chosen. The shell is `min(130ch, 100% - 2rem)`, so it only
     reaches 130 cells at a viewport of 1142.58 + 32 = 1174.58px. Below that the shell
     is `100% - 2rem` and a 40-cell rail would eat 40% of it, leaving the prose at
     roughly 45 characters — under the 60-80 band and worse than the 66 it has now.
     74rem is 1184px, the first whole rem above 1174.58px, so at the exact breakpoint
     the prose gets its full 66 characters and not one pixel less. */
  @media (min-width: 74rem) {
    .body {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, calc(var(--content-cols) * 1ch)) minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: calc(var(--gutter-cols) * 1ch);
      align-items: start;
    }

    /* THE RAIL IS NOT STICKY, and it is the height that decides it. The four skill
       groups measure 662.28px at this rail's 351.56px, and a sticky element needs its
       `top` plus its own height to fit the viewport or its bottom is unreachable while
       it is parked: 101 + 662 = 763px, which clears a 768px laptop by five pixels and
       fails in anything shorter. A sidebar that cannot be scrolled to the end is worse
       than one that scrolls away. The case study's rail keeps its sticky, because a
       table of contents is short and staying put is the whole point of one. */
  }

  /* ---- The hero --------------------------------------------------------------
     Unframed, because this is the reading part of the page and the frame belongs to the
     chrome. The name is the only 52px on the site: it is the one line that has to
     carry the page, and a second line at that size would stop it being the first.

     The hero spans BOTH columns and splits them itself, which is what lets the
     photograph and the name stop competing. Before, they shared 88 cells and the
     arithmetic was brutal:

        portrait 360px / 8.7891px              = 41.0 cells
        name     13 chars at 52px             = 45.1 cells
        gutter                                 =  2.0 cells
                                            -----------
                                                88.1 cells  -> over by 0.1 cell

     A tenth of a cell under a pixel, and the 52px name therefore WRAPPED, and the
     photograph had to be capped to 38ch to buy back 25.8px of slack. Now the portrait
     has the 40-cell rail to itself and the name has all 88, so the cap is gone, the
     wrap is gone, and neither element is diminished to accommodate the other.

     The portrait is the second DOM child on purpose, so the name is what a screen
     reader and a crawler read first, and so a visitor who leaves on load never waited
     for the file. That order is the stacked order too: the text is what the page is
     for, and the photograph is context for it. The two-column split is `grid-area`,
     never `order`, so no width reorders the document for anyone reading it linearly.

     THE PORTRAIT SPANS BOTH ROWS, and that is what removes the hole. Placed in row 1
     alone, row 1 is as tall as the photograph — 351.56px wide at 4:5 is 439.45px tall —
     and the three hero lines are 165px, so the text column carried 274px of nothing.
     Measured on a build, not estimated. Letting the portrait span rows 1 and 2 means
     row 1 is only as tall as the text and the prose starts immediately under it, at
     the same 66ch, beside the picture instead of below it. The DOM order is untouched:
     the text still precedes the photograph, and the prose still follows both. */
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    row-gap: 2ch;
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 74rem) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, calc(var(--content-cols) * 1ch)) minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: calc(var(--gutter-cols) * 1ch);
    }

    .hero__portrait {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1 / span 2;
    }

    /* Explicit, so it lands in row 1 column 1 rather than being auto-placed past the
       spanning portrait. */
    .hero__text {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .intro__body {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
    }
  }

  .hero__text {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The photograph, framed in the same language as everything else and by a completely
     different mechanism.

     NOT IN A `Panel`, and the reason is structural rather than stylistic. A panel's
     frame is real box-drawing TEXT sitting on a character grid: the top and bottom
     rails are runs of `─` and the sides are columns of `│`, both measured in `ch` and
     both depending on the font's 600/1000 advance and on `line-height: 1.5`. An image
     has no character grid. It has no advance, no line box, and no cell to sit in, so a
     photo inside a character frame either opens a gap in the rails or sits visibly off
     the ones it does not break — and nothing about the result names its own cause. So
     the three cues a panel gets are restated in the only language an `<img>` has:

       surface  `--surface-panel` (surface0), the rung every card and panel sits on.
                The hero itself is unframed on `base`, so surface0 is the next step
                up and the photograph lands on the page the way every other raised
                element does.
       border   `--c-frame`, 1px. This is the exact token the panel rails are drawn
                in, it resolves to overlay0, and overlay0 is documented as
                decorative-only — which is what a frame is. It is never a text colour
                here, and verify-structure.mjs checks that.
       radius   2px. `╭ ╮ ╰ ╯` each carry a 0.1 em arm on the 600 advance, and at the
                panel's 15px that arm is 1.5px, so 2px is the closest whole-pixel
                radius that echoes it. It is also the radius `:not(pre) > code` already
                uses, so this is the site's rounding and not a new one. 4px and up
                stops reading as a corner echo and starts reading as a rounded card,
                which is a different shape of thing.

     `width` and `height` on the element are the 360x450 box the browser reserves, and
     the aspect ratio it derives from them is what keeps the page from shifting. There
     is no `max-width` here any more and that is the point of the whole change: the
     track is 40 cells, the image fills it, and nothing has to negotiate. 351.56px out
     of a 720px file is 2.05x, so it is still sharper than the display can resolve. No
     `object-fit` is needed and none is wanted — the file is already cropped 4:5 and
     the box is 4:5, so any fit rule here would be a crop the owner did not ask for. */
  .hero__portrait {
    width: 100%;
    background-color: var(--surface-panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-frame);
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .hero__name {
    font-size: var(--text-hero);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  /* 24px and the accent, which is the same step a card title takes. The role is a
     label, not a headline, and letting it grow with the display scale would put two
     competing headlines at the top of the page. */
  .hero__role {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--c-accent);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  /* The tagline, still the one line it has always been, as the subline under the role.
     Same size as the prose below it and told apart by colour and by a narrower measure
     — a size step here would make the tagline a third headline. */
  .hero__tagline {
    margin-block-start: 0.7em;
    font-size: var(--text-prose);
    line-height: var(--leading-prose);
    color: var(--c-text-soft);
    max-width: var(--measure-lede);
  }

  /* The running prose, in the content column and at the content column's own width.
     `--measure-prose` and `--content-cols` resolve to the same 773.44px here — that is
     the whole reason the rail was sized at 40 cells and not 42 — so the cap never bites
     and the text ends exactly on the frame's right edge rather than 52px short of it. */
  .intro__body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    row-gap: 1.25em;
  }

  .intro__para {
    font-size: var(--text-prose);
    line-height: var(--leading-prose);
    color: var(--c-text);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  }

  /* Three tracks, not a sentence with a `│` in it. That is the whole trick behind the
     wrapped continuation: the text is its own grid item, so when it wraps at the
     measure, the second line starts under the FIRST CHARACTER of the text instead of
     under the label. A `│` between two inline spans would wrap to column zero.

     `--measure-prose` here is 66 characters OF THIS LINE'S OWN SIZE, not 66 characters
     of prose: `ch` resolves against the element, and this line is 14px, so the cap is
     541px rather than the 773px the 20px column gets. That is the right answer for a
     chrome line — a 14px sentence running the full width of a 773px column is 100
     characters and unreadable — and it is stated here because "66ch" reads like a
     promise about the prose measure and is not one on this element. */
  .now {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content max-content minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: baseline;
    margin-block-start: 1.6em;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  }

  .now__label {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
  }

  .now__sep {
    color: var(--c-frame);
    margin-inline: 0.5ch;
  }

  /* ---- Skills -----------------------------------------------------------------
     The same markup and the same styles as the About page's skills section, because it
     is the same data rendered in the same place. Unframed, like that one: a panel
     wrapped around 26 items in a 351.56px rail would be a list in a box, and the frame
     is for values, not for a taxonomy.

     `auto-fit` with a `ch` floor rather than a fixed column count. At 351.56px the
     30ch floor (263.5px) admits one column, so this rail is a single wrapped list per
     group; widen the rail past 60ch and it becomes two without anyone editing it. The
     floor is the chrome's `ch` at 15px, which is the grid the `#` markers are counted
     in, so an item never gets a column too narrow for its own longest word. */
  .groups {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(30ch, 1fr));
    gap: 1.6ch 3ch;
    margin-block-start: 1.4em;
    margin-block-end: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The group headings carry the `.label` utility rather than a local copy of it: the
     rail is unframed, so the surface is `base`, where `subtext0` measures 7.37:1 and the
     utility is safe. It gives the same uppercase-and-tracked treatment the section
     titles use, one step down. */
  .group__items {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.3em 1.5ch;
    margin-block-start: 0.7em;
    margin-block-end: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* The same `#` prefix the project cards and the role stacks use for a tool, so a
     wrapped row of skills cannot be misread as a run of prose. It is inline, next to
     its own word, so it is not announced as a list marker and needs no `aria-hidden`
     span of its own.

     It was `--c-line`, which is overlay2 — the same grey as every quiet rule on the
     site. That made the one marker repeated on all 26 skills the least noticeable thing
     on the page, on a rail whose only job is to be scannable. `--c-marker` is teal, the
     palette's own tooling accent, and it measures 11.01 on base and 8.44 on a card. */
  .group__item::before {
    content: '#';
    color: var(--c-marker);
    margin-inline-end: 0.35ch;
  }

  /* ---- Projects --------------------------------------------------------------
     One column, full width: at 88 shell cells and 20px prose, two columns would give
     each summary about 31 characters, and 31 characters is not a sentence. The card
     explains the rest. `role="list"` is not decoration — `list-style: none` makes Safari
     drop list semantics entirely, and this list is the page's content. */
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    row-gap: 2ch;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* For whoever runs the site, not for whoever reads it. */
  .cards__note {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }
</style>
